message.content:
Below is a quick “starter‑kit” showing exactly what you asked for – how to grab a **reactive** queue from Redisson whose name is `"taskQueue"` while telling Redisson to serialize its elements with your own JSON codec (`jsonCodec`).  
I’ll give you both the *concept* (what’s happening under the hood) and a ready‑to‑paste Java/Kotlin snippet so you can drop it into your project right away.

---

### 📌 What happens behind the scenes?

| Step | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **Create a `RedissonReactiveClient`.** | Gives you access to all reactive APIs (`getDeque`, `getList`, …). |
| **Instantiate your codec (`jsonCodec`)** | Tells Redisson which serializer/deserializer to use when marshalling/unmarshaling items stored in Redis. |
| **Call `redisson.getDeque(“taskQueue”, codec)`** | Returns an `RDequeReactive<T>` backed by the key `taskQueue` in Redis, wired up to use the supplied codec. |

That single line gives you a fully functional reactive queue where each element will automatically be converted to/from JSON whenever you put/get data.

---

### 🛠️ Code Example (Java)

```java
// 1️⃣ Imports -------------------------------------------------------------
import org.redisson.api.RDequeReactive;
import org.redisson.api.RedissonReactiveClient;
import org.redisson.config.Config;
import org.redisson.codec.Codec;
import org.redisson.codec.JsonJacksonCodec;   // <-- our custom JSON codec
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;
import reactor.core.publisher.Flux;

// 2️⃣ Create a RedissonReactiveClient ------------------------------------
Config config = new Config();
// e.g., connect to localhost:6379 – adjust per your environment
config.useSingleServer()
      .setAddress("redis://127.0.0.1:6379");

RedissonReactiveClient redisson = Redisson.create(config).getReactive();

// 3️⃣ Instantiate your codec --------------------------------------------
Codec jsonCodec = new JsonJacksonCodec();     // this uses Jackson under the hood

// 4️⃣ Grab the reactive queue -------------------------------------------
String queueName = "taskQueue";
RDequeReactive<Object> taskQueue =
        redisson.<Object>getDeque(queueName, jsonCodec);    // <- here!

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Now you can work with the queue reactively.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------

class MyJob {
    public int id;
    public String payload;
}

// Add a job
MyJob jobToAdd = new MyJob();
jobToAdd.id = 42;
jobToAdd.payload = "Hello world!";

Mono<Boolean> added$ = taskQueue.offer(jobToAdd);
// subscribe / block / chain whatever fits your flow.

// Consume jobs one at a time
Flux<MyJob> consumer$ = taskQueue.poll(MyJob.class);   // poll until empty
consumer$.subscribe(
    j -> System.out.println("Got job #" + j.id),
    err -> System.err.println(err),
    () -> System.out.println("No more jobs.")
);
```

#### Notes

* Replace `<Object>` with the concrete class you actually store (e.g., `MyJob.class`).  
* If you’re using Spring Boot, you can autowire `RedissonReactiveClient` instead of building it manually.  
* Make sure you’ve got the following Maven dependencies (or Gradle equivalents):

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.redisson</groupId>
    <artifactId>redisson-reactive</artifactId>
    <version>${redisson.version}</version>
</dependency>

<!-- Optional: Jackson codec -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
    <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
    <version>${jackson.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

---

### 🔧 Quick Kotlin Variant

If you prefer Kotlin, the same logic looks like this:

```kotlin
val config = Config().apply { 
    useSingleServer().address = "redis://localhost:6379"
}
val redisson = Redisson.create(config).reactive()

val jsonCodec = JsonJacksonCodec()
val taskQueue: RDequeReactive<MyJob> = redisson.getDeque("taskQueue", jsonCodec)

// Push & pull
taskQueue.offer(MyJob(id = 99, payload = "Kotlin")).blockLast()
taskQueue.poll(MyJob::class.java).doOnNext { println(it) }.blockLast()
```

---

### 🎯 TL;DR

Just call  

```java
RDequeReactive<?> q = redisson.getDeque("taskQueue", new JsonJacksonCodec());
```

and you’re set—your queue will read/write JSON via Jackson without any extra plumbing. Happy coding!

